# Semantic core

This document has two layers, and they are maintained differently:

- **Generated layer — the facts.** One table per route family, rebuilt from `site/content-facts.json` and the built `site/dist/` by `node scripts/semantic-core.mjs` (run from `site/`). It is never edited by hand: regeneration rewrites only the block between the `semantic-core` markers below. The Opening column reuses the content gate's own query rules (`h1-query`, `query-opening` in `site/scripts/content-rows.mjs`), so a tick here means the same thing as green there.
- **Hand-authored layer — the why.** Everything below the markers: why each family exists, the strategy of each family, and the buyer prompts mapped to the route that must win each one, with the passage that answers it and the business reason. Regeneration leaves all of it intact.

`node scripts/semantic-core.mjs --check` recomputes the generated table in memory and fails, naming every drifted route, if it differs from the committed block. The same check rides `gate:content`. It also verifies that every `targetQuery` row maps to a built route, that every gated built route has a row, and that no two routes declare the same target query.

The block between the markers is empty until the first build. Build the site, then run `node scripts/semantic-core.mjs` from `site/` and commit the result.

### Ungated utility routes

| Route         | Why it carries no generated row                                                              | Robots              |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |
| `/404`        | Error page. There is no target query and no citable content.                                 | `noindex, nofollow` |
| `/search`     | Client-side search shell. The results are built in the browser from the static search index. | `noindex, follow`   |
| `/styleguide` | Internal component reference, not a page written to answer a buyer query.                    | `noindex, nofollow` |

